+# Fork
+
+Run a side investigation in a separate pi process with a snapshot of the current
+conversation. The parent continues working and receives a `fork-result` message
+when the investigation finishes.
+
+Add the absolute path to `index.ts` to `extensions` in your agent directory's
+`settings.json` (normally `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`):
+
+```json
+{
+  "extensions": ["/absolute/path/to/fork/index.ts"]
+}
+```
+
+Install through settings, rather than `pi -e`, so the child loads the same
+extension. Requires Node 22 and pi 0.85.1. The extension briefly wraps the public
+provider's stream method to capture the final request after all context hooks;
+the original method is restored before it runs.
+
+The model calls `fork({ "task": "Check whether the other call sites have the same bug" })`.
+The tool immediately returns a unique `forkId`. The child inherits the current
+model, thinking level, environment, effective system prompt, and model context
+(including compaction). Every tool call in the forking assistant message gets a
+synthetic result in the child: these calls belong to the parent and are not
+replayed. The literal task is the child's only new instruction. Forks cannot
+call `fork` themselves.
+
+Each fork saves a session beside its parent's, with `parentSession` in its header.
+Results include the fork id, completion status, session path, and any failure
+reason. Escape does not discard a pending result. `/reload` preserves forks;
+quitting or replacing the parent session stops them. A worker watches a pipe
+owned by the parent and kills the child if the parent dies, including SIGKILL.
+The watchdog starts before the CLI loads extensions and works even when the
+child's main thread is blocked.
+
+Forks share the working directory and filesystem. Tasks should account for
+concurrent edits. Each fork uses the parent's configured providers and incurs
+its own model usage.
+
+Offline tests:
+
+```sh
+npm test -w packages/coding-agent -- test/fork-extension.test.ts
+```
